Context: Beaver's embedded terminal showed white-only text where the same
commands are colored in a system terminal. Investigating that led to three
further defects on the same screen. All four are fixed here; they share no
code with codex/ajout-gemini-glm-astra (checked before starting: no file
overlap).

--- 1. No colors in the shell ---

Root cause: portable_pty::CommandBuilder::new() snapshots std::env::vars_os()
and hands the whole thing to the child. Beaver only overrode TERM. So every
variable of whatever process launched Beaver reached the shell, including
NO_COLOR=1 - set here by the coding agent that ran `npm run tauri dev`, but
equally set by CI runners, by `npm run` under some configs, and by users who
export it in their profile. ls, npm, cargo, git and anything honoring
no-color.org then printed plain text, correctly, on a terminal that is in
fact a 256-color emulator.

Product decision: the terminal declares its own capability rather than
relaying the launcher's directive. NO_COLOR describes the launcher's stdout,
not Beaver's xterm surface; relaying it is a category error. A user who
wants a colorless shell can still `export NO_COLOR=1` inside it.

Fix: new src-tauri/src/services/terminal/shell_environment.rs, the single
authority for what the shell receives. Two lists: DECLARED
(TERM=xterm-256color, COLORTERM=truecolor) and DROPPED (NO_COLOR,
FORCE_COLOR, COLOR, CLICOLOR, CLICOLOR_FORCE). Removals run before
declarations so a name in both lists ends up declared, and a test pins that
order. Both platform files - pty_session_unix.rs and pty_session_windows.rs,
which each set TERM separately until now - go through it via a small
ShellEnvironment trait implemented for portable_pty::CommandBuilder and for
windows_spawn::Command. Nothing else in the environment is touched: PATH,
HOME and the rest still traverse intact, and a test pins that too.

--- 2. Clipped descenders and two glyph widths ---

Root cause: xterm's CharSizeService measures the cell once at open() and
re-measures only on a fontFamily or fontSize change. It never listens to
document.fonts. Beaver names its web font first in --font-mono, so at mount
the cell was measured on the system fallback, then the web font arrived and
was rendered into a cell sized for another face: j, g, p, q, y were clipped
at the baseline and column alignment drifted.

Fix: src/components/terminal/terminal-font.ts. resolveTerminalFont() gives
xterm only the font it actually renders - the fallback part of the stack
while the web font is missing, the full stack once document.fonts.load
resolves. The family string genuinely differs between the two states, which
is what makes xterm re-measure (its OptionsService fires nothing when a
setter receives an identical value). Rejected alternatives: awaiting the
font before term.open() would make the mount effect async, delaying the PTY
spawn and breaking five test files; poking the private CharSizeService would
tie us to xterm internals.

--- 3. Selection painted xterm's factory white ---

Root cause: --term-selection is built with color-mix, which computes to
`color(srgb 1 0.541176 0.298039 / 0.32)` in both Chromium and WebKit. xterm's
css.toColor parses only #rgb[a], #rrggbb[aa] and comma-separated rgb()/rgba();
everything else goes through a canvas litmus test that throws on any alpha
below 0xFF. The throw is swallowed and the selection fell back to
rgba(255,255,255,0.3), identical in every theme and every palette.

Fix: toXtermColor() in terminal-theme.ts rewrites space-separated color()
and rgb() forms into the comma-separated form xterm parses natively, scaling
0-1 channels to 0-255 and accepting a percentage alpha. Anything unrecognized
is returned untouched, so no previously working value changes. A test asserts
the output against a verbatim copy of xterm's own regex.

--- 4. Theme observer blind to the palette ---

use-terminal-theme.ts watched data-theme only. Beaver's accent palette lives
in data-palette, so switching palette left the terminal on the previous
colors until the next theme change. Both attributes are now in the
attributeFilter.

--- Verification ---

Mutation check (both directions): restoring `command.env("TERM",
"xterm-256color")` in pty_session_unix.rs makes
terminal_does_not_relay_the_launcher_refusal_of_color fail with
`left: Some("1") right: None`. Restored afterwards.

End-to-end proof, not just unit: the_launched_shell_never_receives_the_
launcher_refusal_of_color spawns a real PTY and has the shell print
`${NO_COLOR:-absent}_$COLORTERM`. The marker is built by arithmetic
expansion (BEAVER$((21+21))_) so the shell's echo of the typed command
cannot match it, and only a complete line counts as a report.

